Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:07.520
The final step of this pipeline is to assess&nbsp;
how good our model is through performance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:13.680
evaluation. Evaluation of predictive models is&nbsp;
one of the most crucial steps in the pipeline.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:18.320
The basic idea is to develop the&nbsp;
model using some training samples,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:22.960
but test this train model on some other&nbsp;
unseen samples, ideally from future data.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:28.880
It is important to note that the training error&nbsp;
is not very useful, because you can very easily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:34.080
over fit the training data by using complex models&nbsp;
which do not generalize well to future samples.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:38.800
Testing error is the key metric because&nbsp;
it’s a better approximation of the true&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:41.040
performance of the model on future samples.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:46.480
The classical approach for evaluation is&nbsp;
through cross-validation process or CV.

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:51.840
The main idea behind cross-validation&nbsp;
is to iteratively split a data set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:57.040
into training and validation sets. And we&nbsp;
want to view the model on the training set,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:01.440
and test the model on the validation&nbsp;
step, but do this iteratively, many times.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:06.240
Finally, the performance matrix are&nbsp;
aggregated across this iterations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:11.200
often by taking the average. There are&nbsp;
three common methods for cross-validations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:16.880
namely Leave-1-out cross-validation, k-fold&nbsp;
cross-validation, and randomized cross-validation.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:22.880
In Leave-1-Out cross validation, we take&nbsp;
one example at a time as our validation set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:26.000
and use the remaining set as the training set.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:30.000
Then repeat this process many times,&nbsp;
goes through the entire data set.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:35.840
The final performance is computed by averaging&nbsp;
the prediction performance across all iterations.

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:42.320
K-Fold’s cross variation is very similar&nbsp;
to leave-1-out validation. But instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:47.200
just using one example of validation set, we&nbsp;
have multiple samples in the validation set.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:54.080
More specifically, we split the entire data&nbsp;
set into K-Folds. And we iteratively choose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:58.640
each fold as set, validation set and use&nbsp;
the remaining Folds as a trimming set.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:04.480
For example, the Fold 1 would be used as the&nbsp;
validation set, and the remaining fold will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:10.160
be used as a trimming set to view the model.&nbsp;
Then we use a Fold 2 as the validation set,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:15.600
the remaining fold as the training set to be&nbsp;
build another model. And repeat this process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:20.000
K times, and the final performance is the&nbsp;
average over this four different models.

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:26.320
Finally, randomized cross validation will randomly&nbsp;
split the data set into training and testing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:32.400
For each such split, the model is fit to the&nbsp;
training data set, and the prediction accuracy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:37.600
is assessed using the validation set. The&nbsp;
result are then averaged over all the splits.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:42.400
The advantage of this method over the&nbsp;
K-fold cross validation is that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:46.400
proportion of the training and validation&nbsp;
set is not depends on the number of fold.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:51.280
The disadvantage of this method is that&nbsp;
some observation may never be selected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:56.560
Into the validation set because there’s&nbsp;
randomization process. Whereas, some other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:02.560
samples may be selected more than ones into&nbsp;
the validation set. validation set may overlap.

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:08.080
To conclude, in this lecture, we introduced&nbsp;
the key steps in building a predictive model.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:13.600
Which include define what is the prediction&nbsp;
target and construct the right patient cohort,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:18.560
then construct all the possible relevant features&nbsp;
from data, then find which features are relevant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:26.480
and view the predictive model, and&nbsp;
finally, evaluate the model performance.

